1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,830
Tomorrow morning,

2
00:00:00,860 --> 00:00:01,890
the Sun sneezes.

3
00:00:02,220 --> 00:00:02,890
Right at us.

4
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:05,450
The biggest solar storm ever recorded,

5
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:07,390
hitting a planet that runs on electricity.

6
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:09,070
What breaks first?

7
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:10,090
How long does it last?

8
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:11,490
And should you actually be worried?

9
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:12,910
Let's find out,

10
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:14,100
starting with the scary part:

11
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:15,070
how much warning we get.

12
00:00:15,990 --> 00:00:17,210
What if the biggest solar storm

13
00:00:17,210 --> 00:00:18,620
in history hit Earth tomorrow?

14
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:21,480
When the Sun throws a giant

15
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:23,020
cloud of charged gas at us,

16
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:25,350
it has to cross ninety-three million miles of space.

17
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:27,050
The record holder,

18
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:28,310
back in 1859,

19
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:30,630
made that trip in about seventeen hours.

20
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:33,650
Our last alarm is a satellite a million miles out,

21
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:35,070
parked between us and the Sun,

22
00:00:35,070 --> 00:00:36,040
like a doorbell camera.

23
00:00:36,810 --> 00:00:37,710
When the cloud hits it,

24
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:38,690
we get somewhere between fifteen

25
00:00:38,690 --> 00:00:39,940
minutes and an hour.

26
00:00:40,510 --> 00:00:42,180
That's less time than a pizza delivery.

27
00:00:42,770 --> 00:00:44,780
And the pizza isn't trying to fry the power grid.

28
00:00:46,470 --> 00:00:47,360
And yes,

29
00:00:47,510 --> 00:00:48,680
this has happened before.

30
00:00:49,180 --> 00:00:50,930
September 1859.

31
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:52,900
An astronomer named Richard Carrington sees

32
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:54,290
a white flash on the Sun.

33
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:55,630
The next night,

34
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:57,570
the sky explodes with color.

35
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,050
People in the Caribbean see the northern lights.

36
00:01:00,630 --> 00:01:02,320
And the internet of 1859,

37
00:01:02,370 --> 00:01:02,960
the telegraph,

38
00:01:03,090 --> 00:01:04,400
goes completely haywire.

39
00:01:04,910 --> 00:01:06,480
Telegraph machines throw sparks.

40
00:01:06,990 --> 00:01:08,350
One operator in Washington gets

41
00:01:08,350 --> 00:01:09,580
zapped right on the forehead.

42
00:01:10,210 --> 00:01:11,790
Some operators unplug their batteries

43
00:01:11,790 --> 00:01:13,520
and keep sending messages anyway,

44
00:01:13,610 --> 00:01:14,900
powered by the storm itself.

45
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:16,680
Free electricity from space.

46
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:17,910
Sounds great.

47
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:20,480
Until you remember that in 1859,

48
00:01:20,590 --> 00:01:21,690
the most advanced thing plugged

49
00:01:21,690 --> 00:01:22,940
in was a telegraph.

50
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:23,830
Today,

51
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:24,590
it's everything.

52
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:26,480
So,

53
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:27,110
tomorrow.

54
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:29,070
The first things to go are up in space.

55
00:01:29,870 --> 00:01:30,590
In 2022,

56
00:01:30,590 --> 00:01:32,050
a storm that scientists called

57
00:01:32,050 --> 00:01:34,470
minor knocked thirty-eight brand-new Starlink

58
00:01:34,470 --> 00:01:35,820
satellites out of the sky.

59
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:37,130
A minor one.

60
00:01:37,690 --> 00:01:38,820
Then there is GPS.

61
00:01:39,310 --> 00:01:40,050
In May 2024,

62
00:01:40,050 --> 00:01:42,430
a strong storm threw GPS-guided tractors

63
00:01:42,430 --> 00:01:44,400
in North Dakota off by about ten feet.

64
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:46,470
Some farmers just stopped planting.

65
00:01:46,990 --> 00:01:47,530
Tomorrow,

66
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:48,450
your maps app tells you,

67
00:01:48,450 --> 00:01:49,210
with total confidence,

68
00:01:49,210 --> 00:01:50,760
to turn left into a lake.

69
00:01:51,450 --> 00:01:52,400
Planes reroute.

70
00:01:52,890 --> 00:01:53,880
Radio turns to static.

71
00:01:54,030 --> 00:01:55,320
But your phone still works.

72
00:01:55,550 --> 00:01:56,340
For now.

73
00:01:57,720 --> 00:01:59,200
Because the real danger is down here,

74
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:00,290
in the power lines.

75
00:02:00,990 --> 00:02:02,830
The storm shakes Earth's magnetic field,

76
00:02:02,830 --> 00:02:04,110
and that shoves extra current

77
00:02:04,110 --> 00:02:05,170
into long power lines,

78
00:02:05,170 --> 00:02:06,490
like a flood into pipes

79
00:02:06,490 --> 00:02:07,620
that are way too small.

80
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:09,670
In March 1989,

81
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:11,650
a much weaker storm hit Quebec.

82
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:14,290
The whole grid collapsed in about ninety seconds.

83
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:17,320
Six million people lost power for nine hours.

84
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:19,570
And tomorrow's storm is the big one.

85
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:23,550
Here is the part that keeps engineers up at night:

86
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:25,310
the giant transformers.

87
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:27,470
Each one is about the size of a house,

88
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:28,270
built to order,

89
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:30,130
and a new one can take years to arrive.

90
00:02:31,030 --> 00:02:32,930
The insurer Lloyd's ran the numbers

91
00:02:32,930 --> 00:02:34,860
for a storm like 1859.

92
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:37,390
Twenty to forty million Americans without power.

93
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:38,060
In the worst places,

94
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:39,410
for up to a year or two.

95
00:02:40,030 --> 00:02:40,540
The bill:

96
00:02:40,590 --> 00:02:42,784
up to 2.6 trillion dollars.

97
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:44,410
Your fridge would be the first to know.

98
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:45,690
Then your nose.

99
00:02:47,350 --> 00:02:47,670
So,

100
00:02:47,670 --> 00:02:48,520
should you be worried?

101
00:02:49,010 --> 00:02:50,020
For a storm this big,

102
00:02:50,110 --> 00:02:51,890
scientists put the odds anywhere from under

103
00:02:51,890 --> 00:02:53,430
one percent to about twelve percent,

104
00:02:53,430 --> 00:02:54,240
per decade.

105
00:02:55,070 --> 00:02:56,700
And we already had a near miss.

106
00:02:57,170 --> 00:02:58,140
In July 2012,

107
00:02:58,290 --> 00:03:00,100
the Sun fired a storm just as big.

108
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:01,550
Had it launched about a week earlier,

109
00:03:01,550 --> 00:03:02,280
it would have hit us.

110
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:03,810
The good news:

111
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:05,570
even an hour of warning is useful.

112
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:07,220
Grid operators can lower the load,

113
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:09,270
and satellites can hide in safe mode.

114
00:03:10,030 --> 00:03:10,680
And for you?

115
00:03:10,890 --> 00:03:11,440
A flashlight,

116
00:03:11,570 --> 00:03:11,990
some water,

117
00:03:11,990 --> 00:03:12,720
and a paper map.

118
00:03:13,290 --> 00:03:14,520
Old school beats no school.

119
00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:17,790
And here's the weird upside.

120
00:03:18,290 --> 00:03:19,410
Everyone on Earth gets to

121
00:03:19,410 --> 00:03:20,440
see the northern lights.

122
00:03:20,650 --> 00:03:21,540
Even in Miami.

123
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:23,970
You'd finally get the perfect selfie.

124
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:25,550
You just couldn't post it.

125
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:27,420
Want to see what happens when

126
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:29,130
the internet loses its ocean cables?

127
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:30,090
It's right here.

